------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Change Log  (only recent)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Unreleased
	Fixes for issues #94 to #99, #102, #107, #141 to #144 and #195.
	Results that change:
	  rbp starts the range of its gains at 0, where it read min and
	    max before setting them. Its value no longer depends on the
	    build or on -q, and on graded qrels it can change (#94).
	  Under -c, num_rel on the all row counts only the judgments at
	    or above -l, and only in the topic that -D names (#95).
	  Under -c, a qrels topic missing from the run is evaluated as a
	    ranking that retrieves nothing, where it was scored on a
	    placeholder document and some rows were then reset. utility,
	    rbp_resid and relstring with parameters, and gain maps that
	    give level -1 a gain, change for such a topic:
	    utility.1,-1,0,0 gives 0 instead of -1, rbp_resid.p=0.5 0
	    instead of 1, relstring.3 '' instead of '-', and ndcg.-1=1 0
	    instead of 1. utility with a nonzero third or fourth
	    coefficient gives p3 * num_rel + p4 * (N - num_rel), where N
	    is the -N value (#96).
	  A topic whose grades are all negative is evaluated with its
	    own grade counts. It no longer exits with status 4 or
	    crashes, and ndcg, ideal_dcg, ndcg_rel and G with a gain on
	    level -2 no longer use the previous topic's ideal ranking
	    (#97).
	  ndcg, ideal_dcg, Rndcg, ndcg_rel and G give the right value
	    with gains less than 1 apart: ndcg.1=0.3,2=0.6,3=0.9 gives
	    0.9225, the value of ndcg.1=3,2=6,3=9, instead of 0, and
	    ndcg.0=0,1=0.3,2=0.9,3=0.6 gives 1.0000 instead of 1.0840
	    (#98).
	  ndcg_rel gives 0 instead of inf when no ideal gain is positive
	    (#98).
	  dcg, ideal_dcg and ndcg each keep their own gain map. Two of
	    them with a gain map, as in -m all_trec -m dcg.1=3, no
	    longer crash or print one measure with the gains or name of
	    another (#195).
	  11pt_avg sorts its recall levels: 11pt_avg.1.0,0.5 gives the
	    value of 11pt_avg.0.5,1.0 under its own row name
	    (#142 item 5).
	  11pt_avg and iprec_at_recall give 0 instead of nan for a topic
	    that retrieves nothing, as under -J or -M 0, so such a topic
	    no longer makes the all row nan (#142 item 4).
	  Rndcg gives 0 instead of nan for a topic with no positive
	    ideal gain (#142 item 9).
	  Integer cutoffs that differ by 2^31 or more are sorted: with
	    P.4294967297,1, P_1 prints first and is 1.0000, not 1.5000
	    (#142 item 12).
	  -Z converts a value only with the zscores line of its own
	    topic and measure. A missing line gives -1000000.0000, where
	    the line of another topic could be used (#144 item 1).
	  With no -m, -R qrels_jg prints the qrels_jg measures, and
	    -R prefs and -R qrels_prefs print the prefs measures,
	    instead of exiting with status 4 (#141 item 1).
	  bpref and gm_bpref with a very large -l, such as
	    -l 1000000000000, give 0 instead of crashing (#102).
	  A relstring length beyond the number of documents retrieved,
	    such as relstring.1e300, lists every retrieved document on
	    every build. The length was converted to a long with
	    undefined behaviour (#142 item 2).
	Row names that change:
	  Rprec_mult and Rprec_mult_avgjg print a cutoff in full, to two
	    decimals: Rprec_mult.100000000 prints
	    Rprec_mult_100000000.00, not Rprec_mult_10000000
	    (#142 item 14).
	Inputs now refused, with exit status 2:
	  A run score that has no leading number, or is NaN (#107).
	  In a qrels or qrels_jg file, a relevance level outside -127 to
	    127 (#143 item 4). prefs and qrels_prefs files keep no range
	    limit.
	  In a prefs or qrels_prefs file, a level that is not a number
	    or is NaN. Such a level was read as 0, or could crash
	    (#143 item 3).
	  In any input file or standard input, a NUL byte, and a CR that
	    ends neither a line nor the file, a CR between two fields
	    included (#143 items 1, 5, 7 and 10).
	  A qrels, qrels_jg, prefs or qrels_prefs file with no data
	    lines (#143 item 2). A results file of only comments or
	    blank lines, and standard input without data lines, which
	    crashed; an empty results file already exited 2 (#99).
	  In a zscores file, a mean or standard deviation that is not a
	    finite number, and a negative standard deviation
	    (#143 item 12).
	  Measure parameters that cannot be used (#142): in G, dcg,
	    ideal_dcg, ndcg, ndcg_rel, Rndcg, rbp and rbp_resid, a gain
	    name that is not a relevance level or repeats a level, and
	    in rbp and rbp_resid a p given twice (item 6); a
	    floating-point value that is not a number (item 7) or is not
	    finite (item 8); an iprec_at_recall or 11pt_avg recall level
	    outside [0, 1], an Rprec_mult or Rprec_mult_avgjg multiple
	    that is not positive, a negative set_F weight, and an rbp or
	    rbp_resid p outside [0, 1) (item 11); floating-point cutoffs
	    that print the same row name, such as Rprec_mult.0.999,1.001
	    (item 15); and utility without exactly four coefficients,
	    which exited 4 at the first topic (item 16).
	  A gain name beyond the range of a long, such as
	    ndcg.99999999999999999999=1, exits 2 when the measure is
	    initialised, instead of 4 at the first topic (#142 item 6).
	  An unreadable or malformed zscores file exits with status 2
	    after "Quit in file", instead of 255 (#141 item 4).
	Options now refused, with exit status 1:
	  A negative -l, -M or -N (#142 items 1, 10 and 13). A negative
	    -M crashed, and a negative -l counted documents outside the
	    qrels as relevant. -l 0 and -M 0 stay legal.
	Inputs now refused, with exit status 4:
	  Where long has 32 bits, as on Windows, an input whose table of
	    counts has more entries than a long holds, such as a prefs
	    judgment group of 65,536 documents (#143 item 9).
	Inputs read differently or with a new warning:
	  A run score is read with strtod. Characters after the number
	    are ignored with a warning, and a score beyond the range of
	    a double is kept as inf or 0 with a warning (#107,
	    #143 item 13).
	  A prefs or qrels_prefs level, a zscores mean or standard
	    deviation, and a floating-point measure parameter with
	    characters after the number are read with a warning
	    (#142 item 7, #143 items 3 and 12).
	  In qrels, qrels_jg, prefs and qrels_prefs files, a line whose
	    first non-blank character is # is skipped, wherever it is. A
	    comment no longer crashes the reader, stops it with
	    "Malformed line" or becomes a judgment, and an indented #
	    line no longer adds a topic named # (#143 items 2 and 8).
	  A blank line in a qrels, qrels_jg, prefs or qrels_prefs file
	    is skipped with a warning that names the line, instead of
	    "Malformed line" (#143 item 8).
	  A UTF-8 byte-order mark at the start of any input file is
	    skipped (#143 item 6).
	  Only "-" itself reads results from standard input. Another
	    path that starts with "-" is opened as a file
	    (#143 item 11).
	  Results on standard input are read in full, so a NUL is
	    refused as in a file, instead of dropping or joining lines
	    (#143 item 10).
	  On 64-bit builds, judgments and zscores files of 2 GiB or more
	    are read, instead of refused or read in part (#143 item 9).
	Messages:
	  Without -c, a qrels topic missing from the run is reported as
	    "qrels qid '<qid>' not found in results", not "result qid
	    '<qid>' not found in qrels". It is found before any topic is
	    evaluated, so -q and -D print nothing before the exit with
	    status 4 (#141 items 2 and 3).
	  "Malformed line" counts every line of the file, comment and
	    blank lines included, in the qrels, qrels_jg, prefs,
	    qrels_prefs and results readers (#141 item 5, #143 item 14).
	  The duplicate-document messages end with a newline
	    (#141 item 6).
	  Message prefixes name the file that prints them: form_res_rels
	    and form_res_rels_jg replace form_res_qrels,
	    form_prefs_counts replaces form_prefs_info, get_qrels_jg
	    replaces get_qrels, and get_qrels_prefs, with "Cannot read
	    qrels file", replaces get_prefs (#141 item 7).
	  Number conversion warnings and errors name the source in
	    parentheses: the measure, or the line and file, and for a
	    judgment the topic and document (#142 item 17). "Illegal
	    cutoff" no longer follows the error for a cutoff.
	  New messages name the line and file of a NUL, a CR and a
	    relevance level out of range, and name a file without data
	    lines (#99, #143).
	Help text:
	  -h gives the -N default as 0 and the long form of -M as
	    --Max_retrieved_per_topic (#141 item 8).
	  -h says that -c evaluates a missing topic as an empty ranking
	    and that without -c a missing topic is an error, and no
	    longer says that such topics are ignored (#96, #141 item 2).
	  -h says that -l must be 0 or more (#142 item 10), lists the
	    qrels_jg group and the default measures for each -R
	    (#141 item 1), and gives the qrels level range as -127 to
	    127 and the comment and blank-line rules of the four
	    judgment formats (#143 items 4 and 8).
	  The short usage message says that the default measures depend
	    on -R, and no longer says that all_trec prints all measures
	    (#141 item 1).
	  -h -m rbp_resid says that rbp_resid accepts gains and ignores
	    them (#142 item 6).
	Memory errors and leaks, with no change in printed values:
	  bpref and gm_bpref no longer read past their table of grade
	    counts when -l exceeds a topic's highest grade by more than
	    1 (#102).
	  rbp no longer leaks its gain table for each topic, and under
	    -c relstring no longer leaks a buffer for each missing topic
	    (#144 items 2 and 3).
	  Rprec_mult and Rprec_mult_avgjg keep a cutoff as a double, and
	    utility counts the documents not retrieved without overflow,
	    so a huge multiple or a -N near LONG_MAX no longer gives
	    undefined behaviour (#142 items 2 and 3).
	  Malloc, Realloc, the sorts and the rank comparators no longer
	    cut a size, count or rank to 32 bits, which needed 2^31 or
	    more items or 4 GiB or more (#143 item 9, #144 item 4).
	Removed:
	  The function clear_relstr and its declaration in functions.h
	    (#144 item 3).
	  m_ndcg_p.c, which the Makefile did not build (#141 item 9).
	Declarations:
	  In common.h, Malloc and Realloc call te_malloc_array and
	    te_realloc_array, defined in utility_pool.c, and return NULL
	    when n * sizeof(type) does not fit in a size_t
	    (#143 item 9).
	  functions.h declares the reader, number conversion and
	    initialisation functions that the readers and measures now
	    share, comp_double, and MIN_REL_LEVEL and MAX_REL_LEVEL.
	Build:
	  make quicktest also builds and runs three test programs and
	    the scripts in test/regress. make clean removes the
	    programs.
	  CI runs make quicktest with the address, undefined behaviour
	    and float-cast-overflow sanitizers, and as a 32-bit build.
6/18/26 Version 10.0
	Breaking fix: change uses of int truncation to round/floor.
	-c mode now prints out skipped queries in -q mode.
	Added measures to the all_trec set: rbp, rbp_resid, unj_5/10/20
	Support for '#' comment lines in TREC-format runs and qrels files.
       Fixes for GCC 15 and Windows
       Allow results files to be read from stdin
	Added LICENSE: MIT-ish
	Code reformatted
